    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>First Woman as Sole Composer</h4>
      <p>Hildur Guðnadóttir won for <em>Joker</em> (2019), the first woman to win Best Original Score as the sole credited composer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>First Electronic / Synth Score</h4>
      <p>Vangelis won for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981), bringing entirely synthesizer-based scoring into the Oscar mainstream.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Rock Musicians Enter the Category</h4>
      <p>Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross won for <em>The Social Network</em> (2010), the first rock/industrial artists to take the prize, and won again for <em>Soul</em> (2020).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Three-Win Streak</h4>
      <p>Alan Menken won three consecutive years (1989, 1991, 1992) for Disney's animated renaissance — the only composer with a three-peat in this category.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Most Wins in the Dataset</h4>
      <p>John Barry and John Williams each hold {max_wins} wins — Barry for <em>Born Free</em>, <em>The Lion in Winter</em>, <em>Out of Africa</em>, <em>Dances with Wolves</em>; Williams for <em>Jaws</em>, <em>Star Wars</em>, <em>E.T.</em>, <em>Schindler's List</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ═══ 6. CONCLUSIONS ═══ -->
<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">Key <span>Takeaways</span></h2>
  <p class="section-subtitle">Five evidence-based conclusions about the evolution of Oscar-winning scores</p>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>1. Composer dominance has fragmented over time</h4>
    <p>The Golden Age saw a small circle of studio composers capture repeat wins; the Contemporary era has produced far more one-time winners, reflecting the industry's decentralization and the Academy's growing appetite for new voices. Only 2 composers have won more than twice in the 21st century.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>2. The repeat winner rate underscores a structural shift</h4>
    <p>At {repeat_winner_rate}% overall, the repeat winner rate masks a strong temporal gradient: early decades featured repeat winners routinely, while recent decades have seen predominantly first-time winners. The 2020s, in particular, have been dominated by newcomers and one-time winners, with Göransson as the notable exception.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>3. International and genre diversity has accelerated</h4>
    <p>From the European emigres of the 1930s–50s to A.R. Rahman (India), Tan Dun (China), Hildur Guðnadóttir (Iceland), and Volker Bertelmann (Germany), the category has become genuinely global. Simultaneously, the definition of a "score" has expanded from full-orchestral to electronic, ambient, and experimental.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>4. The 1990s–2000s were a peak of turnover</h4>
    <p>The {decade_most_unique} produced the most unique winning composers of any decade, a period that saw the Disney renaissance, the rise of Hans Zimmer's influence, and a parade of international first-time winners. This period effectively ended the era of dominant repeat figures.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>5. Ludwig Göransson is defining the Contemporary era</h4>
    <p>With three wins spanning <em>Black Panther</em> (2018), <em>Oppenheimer</em> (2023/2024), and <em>Sinners</em> (2026), Göransson has become the most decorated composer of the 21st century's first quarter. His blend of orchestral, African, and electronic elements exemplifies where the category is heading — genre-fluid, culturally expansive, and sonically boundary-pushing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

</div>

