  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">Era-Based <span>Analysis</span></h2>
  <p class="section-subtitle">How winning-score profiles and composer archetypes have shifted across four broad historical periods</p>
  {era_sections}
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">Notable <span>Milestones</span> &amp; Records</h2>
  <p class="section-subtitle">Landmark moments, streaks, gaps, and firsts in the Best Original Score category</p>
  <div class="milestone-grid">
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Longest Gap Between Wins</h4>
      <p>{max_gap_info}</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Most Recent Winner</h4>
      <p>Ludwig G&ouml;ransson for <em>Sinners</em> (2026) &mdash; his third Best Original Score Oscar, marking a dominant run in the Contemporary era.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Back-to-Back Repeat Winners</h4>
      <ul>{bb_html}</ul>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>First Woman as Sole Composer</h4>
      <p>Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir won for <em>Joker</em> (2019), the first woman to win Best Original Score as the sole credited composer.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>First Electronic / Synth Score</h4>
      <p>Vangelis won for <em>Chariots of Fire</em> (1981), bringing entirely synthesizer-based scoring into the Oscar mainstream.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Rock Musicians Enter the Category</h4>
      <p>Trent Reznor and Atticus Ross won for <em>The Social Network</em> (2010), the first rock/industrial artists to take the prize, and won again for <em>Soul</em> (2020).</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Three-Win Streak</h4>
      <p>Alan Menken won three consecutive years (1989, 1991, 1992) for Disney's animated renaissance &mdash; the only composer with a three-peat in this category.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="milestone-card">
      <h4>Most Wins in the Dataset</h4>
      <p>John Barry and John Williams each hold {max_wins} wins &mdash; Barry for <em>Born Free</em>, <em>The Lion in Winter</em>, <em>Out of Africa</em>, <em>Dances with Wolves</em>; Williams for <em>Jaws</em>, <em>Star Wars</em>, <em>E.T.</em>, <em>Schindler's List</em>.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">Key <span>Takeaways</span></h2>
  <p class="section-subtitle">Five evidence-based conclusions about the evolution of Oscar-winning scores</p>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>1. Composer dominance has fragmented over time</h4>
    <p>The Golden Age saw a small circle of studio composers capture repeat wins; the Contemporary era has produced far more one-time winners, reflecting the industry's decentralization and the Academy's growing appetite for new voices. Only 2 composers have won more than twice in the 21st century.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>2. The repeat winner rate underscores a structural shift</h4>
    <p>At {repeat_winner_rate}% overall, the repeat winner rate masks a strong temporal gradient: early decades featured repeat winners routinely, while recent decades have seen predominantly first-time winners. The 2020s, in particular, have been dominated by newcomers and one-time winners, with G&ouml;ransson as the notable exception.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>3. International and genre diversity has accelerated</h4>
    <p>From the European emigres of the 1930s&ndash;50s to A.R. Rahman (India), Tan Dun (China), Hildur Gu&eth;nad&oacute;ttir (Iceland), and Volker Bertelmann (Germany), the category has become genuinely global. Simultaneously, the definition of a &ldquo;score&rdquo; has expanded from full-orchestral to electronic, ambient, and experimental.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>4. The {decade_most_unique} were a peak of turnover</h4>
    <p>The {decade_most_unique} produced the most unique winning composers of any decade, a period that saw the Disney renaissance, the rise of Hans Zimmer's influence, and a parade of international first-time winners. This period effectively ended the era of dominant repeat figures.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="takeaway">
    <h4>5. Ludwig G&ouml;ransson is defining the Contemporary era</h4>
    <p>With three wins spanning <em>Black Panther</em> (2018), <em>Oppenheimer</em> (2023/2024), and <em>Sinners</em> (2026), G&ouml;ransson has become the most decorated composer of the 21st century's first quarter. His blend of orchestral, African, and electronic elements exemplifies where the category is heading &mdash; genre-fluid, culturally expansive, and sonically boundary-pushing.</p>
  </div>
</section>

