            "Alfred Newman and Dimitri Tiomkin built prolific careers within the studio apparatus. "
            "Wins were concentrated among a handful of repeat winners: "
            f"{stats['unique_composers']} unique composers across {stats['ceremonies']} ceremonies, "
            "with several figures winning multiple times. The era's scores were overwhelmingly "
            "orchestral, leitmotif-driven, and produced under the studio system's factory-like conditions."
        )
    elif "Transitional" in era_name:
        return (
            "The 1960s and 1970s saw film music diversify dramatically. The European orchestral "
            "tradition gave way to jazz-inflected scores (Henry Mancini, Elmer Bernstein), pop and "
            "rock influences (Burt Bacharach, Giorgio Moroder, Vangelis), and a new generation of "
            "melody-driven composers. Maurice Jarre and John Barry bridged the old and new, winning "
            "with both epic orchestral scores and intimate themes. The era produced the category's "
            "first electronic-score winner (Vangelis, 1981). "
            f"{stats['unique_composers']} unique composers won across {stats['ceremonies']} ceremonies, "
            "reflecting a marked increase in stylistic diversity."
        )
    elif "Modern" in era_name:
        return (
            "The Modern era consolidated the blockbuster scoring style, with John Williams and John Barry "
            "continuing their dominance while new voices emerged. Alan Menken's unprecedented three-win "
            "streak (1989-1992) for Disney animated films reshaped the category. The era also saw the "
            "first jazz artist win (Herbie Hancock, 1986), first Asian-born composer (Ryuichi Sakamoto, "
