<section>
  <h2 class="section-title">Dataset <span>Overview</span></h2>
  <p class="section-subtitle">Scope, key statistics, and historical context for the Academy Award for Best Original Score</p>
  <p>The Academy Award for Best Original Score has been presented since the 6th ceremony in 1934, making it one of the Oscars' longest-running categories. This dataset encompasses <strong>{total_ceremonies} ceremonies</strong> and <strong>{total_unique} distinct composers</strong>, each credited with at least one winning score. Over nine decades, the category has evolved from honoring studio-contracted orchestral arrangers to celebrating globe-spanning, genre-defying artists.</p>
  <p style="margin-top:12px;">The category has undergone several structural changes &mdash; at various points splitting into separate awards for dramatic and comedy/musical scores &mdash; but this analysis follows the consolidated one-winner-per-year convention. Composer names have been standardized; where multiple composers shared an award, each is counted individually.</p>
</section>

<section>
